<section xml:id="section-quotients">
  <title>Quotients</title>
  <subsection>
    <title>Group Quotients</title>
    <p>
      I've talked about group products and extension: ways of putting
      smaller groups together to build larger groups. I want to do the
      opposite here and talk about how to break groups apart into
      smaller pieces. You may think we've already done this with
      subgroups, but this section is going to introduce a new kind of
      subgroup. It turns out, for the structure of groups, not all
      subgroups are equally useful.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>N \subset G</m> be a subgroup. Then <m>N</m> is
          called a <term>normal subgroup</term> if <m>N</m> is the
          kernel of some homomorphism with domain <m>G</m>. The
          notation for a normal subgroup is <m>N \triangleleft G</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      I am again developing a theme I introduced a couple sections ago:
      homomorphisms are the main tool to understand groups. In <xref
      ref="section-homomorphism"/> I defined kernels and images and
      proved that both are subgroups. This new definition of normal
      subgroups is important because not every subgroup can be a kernel
      and being a kernel leads to some very important construction.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        The determinant map <m>\det: GL(n) \rightarrow \RR^*</m> is a
        homomorphism. Its kernel is all matrices with determinant
        <m>1</m>: this is precisely <m>SL(n)</m>. Therefore,
        <m>SL(n)</m> is a normal subgroup. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Reduction modulo <m>n</m> is a homomorphism: <m>\pi_n \ZZ
        \rightarrow \ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. Its kernel is the subgroup of all
        integers which are multiples of <m>n</m>, which is written
        <m>n\ZZ</m>. This is a normal subgroup of <m>\ZZ</m> for any
        <m>n \in \NN</m>. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      The definition I have given is a good definition and relates to
      homomorphisms. However, it is not the only definition. (In fact,
      I'm being a bit unusual here: most group theory texts will give
      an alternative definition and later prove that normal subgroups
      are exactly the kernels of homomorphisms. I'm doing the
      opposite, but I think it is the more sensible approach). In
      particluar, I gave a conceptually good definition, but it is not
      the easiest definition to work with. If I have a particular
      subgroup, it may not be easy to find a homomorphism witht the
      appropriate kernel to prove that it is normal. If it is not
      normal, it may be very difficult to prove that no such
      homomorphism exists. Therefore, I can give some other
      conditions. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>N \subset G</m> be a subgroup. The following are all
          equivalent conditions for <m>N</m> to be a normal subgroup.
          (Equilvalent means that any of these conditions implies all
          the others.) 
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>N</m> is the kernel of some homomorphism with domain
              <m>G</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              For all <m>n \in N</m> and <m>g \in G</m>, <m>gng^{-1}
              \in N</m>. In this case, we say that <m>N</m> is
              invariant under conjugation. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The left cosets of <m>N</m> are the same as the right
              cosets of <m>N</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              The (left) cosets of <m>N</m> form a group under the
              binary operation applied to the representative of the
              coset. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I'm not going to prove all of these equivalence, for the sake
          of detail and time. However, I will prove the most importnat
          one: the fact that the last statement of the proof implies the
          first statement. 
        </p>
        <p>
          So assume that the left cosets of <m>N</m> form a group. A
          coset is written <m>gN</m>, so the binary operation on two
          cosets is 
          <me>
            (g_1N) (g_2N) = (g_1g_2N)
          </me>
          The identity elements in this group of cosets is <m>eN = N</m>
          and the inverse of a coset <m>gN</m> is <m>g^{-1}N</m>. This
          binary operation does not always produce a group, hence the
          definition of a normal subgroup: if this operation on cosets
          works consistently, the subgroup is normal. To prove this,
          using the defintiion I gave, I need to show that, under the
          assumption that the cosests form a group, <m>N</m> is the
          kernel of some homomorphism.
        </p>
        <p>
          I define a homomorphism <m>\pi_N</m> which goes from <m>G</m>
          to this new group forms of the cosets of <m>N</m>. The
          function is defined as 
          <me>
            \phi(g) = gN
          </me>
          The function just sends every element to its cosets. Then the
          homomorphism condition is immediate from the definition of the
          binary operation on cosets: I'm just doing the operation of
          the group on the coset representatives. 
        </p>
        <p>
          What is the kernel of this map? Well, it is everything that is
          sent to the identity, which is the coset <m>eN = N</m>. What
          happens if <m>gN = N</m> for some arbitrary group element? The
          left side includes <m>ge = g</m>, since the identity is always a
          member of any subgroup, so <m>g \in N</m>. And for anything in
          the subgroup, <m>nN = N</m>. Therefore, the kernel is exactly
          the group <m>N</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      In this proof, I have already implicitly defined the major
      construction of this section.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>N \triangleleft G</m> be a normal subgroup. Then the
          group of cosets of <m>N</m> is called the <term>quotient
          group</term> of <m>G</m> by <m>N</m>. The notation for a quotient
          group is <m>G/N</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <example>
      <p>
        There is a very important example that we've already spend a lot
        of time with: <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. <m>n\ZZ</m> is a normal subgroup,
        so this quotient exists. This notation is also finally
        explained: this was always the notation for a quotient group.
      </p>
      <p>
        It's worthwhile to talk through this example in detail. What is
        happening in general in a quotient group is very much a general
        extension of the idea of the integers modulo <m>n</m>. This is
        the conceptual archetype.
      </p>
      <p>
        So what is really happening in the integers modulo <m>n</m>?
        There are several equivalent ways to understand what is going
        on.
        <ul>
          <li>
            By moving to modular arithmetic, I take any integer <m>k</m>
            and consider it only up to addition or subtration by a
            multiple of <m>n</m>. Well, <m>n\ZZ</m> is a normal
            subgroup, so I am identifying <m>k</m> with any other
            integer that differs by an element of a normal subgroup. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Alternatively, I can think of modular arithmetic as
            identifying <m>k</m> with the entire set <m>\bar{k}</m> plus
            all multiples of <m>n</m>. That's the set 
            <me>
              \{k + in | i \in \ZZ\}
            </me>
            But this set is exactly a coset: <m>k + n\ZZ</m> (written
            with addition now since that is the group operation in the
            integers modulo n). 
          </li>
          <li>
            I can understand modular arithmetic as an equivalent
            relation: two integers are equivalent if 
            <me>
              k = l \mod n
            </me>
            Then the intergers modulo <m>n</m> are the equivalence
            classes of this equivalence relation. The equivalence
            relation is defined by the subgroup <m>n\ZZ</m>: things are
            equivalent if and only if they differ by a multiple of
            <m>n</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
        In any way to understand modular arithmetic, I am using a normal
        subgroup to make identifications and working with the resulting
        new set that incorportates the identifications. This is exactly
        what is happening in a group quotient.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the general case, <m>N \triangleleft G</m>, instead of
        working modulo <m>n</m>, I now work modulo <m>N</m>. In the
        quotient, the new elements are the <sq>same</sq> only if they
        differ by something in <m>N</m>. I can treat the new elements as
        cosets <m>gN</m>. It's exactly the same idea of modular
        arithemtic, but must more abstract. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      I previously defined the projection function <m>\pi_n: \ZZ
      \rightarrow \ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. This also extends to the general case. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>N \triangleleft G</m> be a normal subgroup. The
          projection homomorphism 
          <me>
            \pi_N: G \rightarrow G/N
          </me>
          is the homomorphism defined by <m>\phi(g) = gN</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      This was the homormophism I already used in the previous proof:
      the normal subgroup is precisely the kernel of its projection
      homomorphism. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Let me get into some more examples. Quotients are a strange idea,
      so more practice with specific groups is time well spent.
      However, before I get into examples, I'll prove one useful
      proposition.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Any subgroup of an abelian group is normal. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Here I can use the other equivalent definition of a normal
          subgroup. The second statment says that a group is normal if
          it is invariant under conjugation. In an abelian group,
          conjugation does nothing, so all subgroups are invariant
          under conjugation. Therefore, all subgroups are normal.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the subbroup <m>\ZZ \subset \RR</m>. Since these are
        abelian groups (under addition), this is a normal subgroup,
        <m>\ZZ \triangleleft \RR</m>. Therefore, I can define the
        group quotient <m>\RR/\ZZ</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        What is the structure of this group? It is all real numbers
        where any two numbers that differ by an integer are identified
        as the <sq>same</sq>. So all of the real numbers
        <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>\frac{9}{2}</m>, <m>\frac{-13}{2}</m>,
        <m>\frac{141}{2}</m>, and <m>\frac{3105}{2}</m> are consider the
        same in this quotient group because they differ by some whole
        number. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Since I can add or subtract any whole number, I can write any
        element of this quotient as the coset <m>r + \ZZ</m> for <m>r
        \in [0,1)</m>. This is like choosing number is <m>\{0, 1, 2,
        \ldots, n-1\}</m> to represent integers modulo n: I am choosing
        a simple set of representatives. So <m>\RR/\ZZ</m> is
        something like the interval <m>[0,1)</m>. But what happens if
        I add together two of these cosets? Take this, for example.
        <me>
          \left( \frac{4}{5} + \ZZ \right) + \left( \frac{5}{7} + \ZZ
          \right) = \left(\frac{4}{5} + \frac{5}{7} \right) + \ZZ =
          \left( \frac{28 + 25}{35} \right) + \ZZ = \frac{53}{35} +
          \ZZ
        </me>
        Then I can reduce this by subtracting 1: any adjustment by a
        whole number doesn't change the set. 
        <me>
          \frac{53}{35} + \ZZ = \frac{53 - 35}{35} + \ZZ =
          \frac{18}{35} + \ZZ
        </me>
        This gives me a representative in <m>[0,1)</m>. What I have
        done here is essentially looped around: when I go past
        <m>1</m> in this inteval, I subtract <m>1</m> and start again
        at <m>0</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        So this quotient group is something like the interval
        <m>[0,1)</m> where, when I go past the end of the interval, I
        loop around to the start. If I were to do this visually, I
        would take the interval, curve is around an attach <m>0</m> to
        <m>1</m>. I would get a circle.
      </p>
      <p>
        And, indeed, this is called the <term>cicle group</term> in
        group theory. It is the standard group structure on a circle. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consdier the subgroup <m>\langle F_1 \rangle = \{\Id, F_1\}
        \subset D_4</m>.  I calculated the conjugacy classes of
        <m>D_4</m> in <xref ref="example-d4-conjugacy-classes"/>.
        <m>\{F_1, F_3\}</m> was a conjugacy classes: some of the
        conjugates of <m>F_1</m> are <m>F_3</m>. But <m>F_3</m> is not
        in the subgroup; therefore, this subgroup is not invariant
        under conjugation. This is not a normal subgroup so I cannot
        make a group quotient. If I tried, the cosets would fail to
        produce a group structure: the binary operation would not
        work. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Now consdier the subgroup <m>\langle R_2 \rangle = \{\Id,
        R_2\} \subset D_4</m>.  <m>\{R_2\}</m> was a conjugacy class
        of <m>D_4</m>, so all conjugates of <m>R_2</m> are just
        <m>R_2</m>. This means that the subgroup <m>\langle R_2
        \rangle</m> is invariant under conjugation, so it is a normal
        subgroup. I can form the group quotient <m>D_4/\langle R_2
        \rangle</m>. It consists of these four cosets (which I
        calculate via the Caley table.) 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \Id \langle R_2 \rangle = \{ \Id, R_2 \}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            R_1 \langle R_2 \rangle = \{ R_1, R_3 \}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            F_1 \langle R_2 \rangle = \{ F_1, F_3 \}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            F_2 \langle R_2 \rangle = \{ F_2, F_4 \}
          </mrow>
        </md>
        I can perform arithmetic on these cosets. For example. 
        <me>
          \left( F_2 \langle R_2 \rangle \right) 
          \left( F_1 \langle R_2 \rangle \right) =  
          F_2F_1 \langle R_2 \rangle = 
          R_1 \langle R_2 \rangle \
        </me>
        What I produce is a new group of order 4.
      </p>
      <p>
        What is this group structure, you might ask? It is isomorphic
        to <m>\ZZ/2\ZZ \times \ZZ/2\ZZ</m>, which means it is not
        cyclic but it is abelian. The isomorphism is given by this
        table, which you are free to verify if you wish.
        <me>
          \begin{array}{c|cccc}
            g \amp \Id \langle R_2 \rangle \amp R_1 \langle R_2
            \rangle \amp F_1 \langle R_2 \rangle \amp F_2 \langle R_2
            \rangle \\
            \phi(g) \amp (0,0) \amp (1,0) \amp (0,1) \amp (1,1)
          \end{array}
        </me>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider <m>\ZZ/25\ZZ = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots 24\}</m> expressed
        as numbers modulo <m>25</m>. <m>\langle 5 \rangle = \{0, 5,
        10, 15, 20\}</m> is a subgroup. It is a normal subgroup since
        this is an abelian, where everything is normal. Therefore, the
        cosets form a quotient group. Here are those cosets.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            0 + \langle 5 \rangle 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            1 + \langle 5 \rangle 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2 + \langle 5 \rangle 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            3 + \langle 5 \rangle 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            4 + \langle 5 \rangle 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        This quotient acts excatly like the integers modulo 5, even
        though it sits inside the integers modulo 25. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          I can generalize the previous examples. Let <m>n,m \in
          \ZZ</m> and assume that <m>m</m> is a factor of <m>n</m>.
          Then <m>\langle m \rangle</m> is a normal subgroup of
          <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> and the following isormorphism exists.
          <me>
            \left( \ZZ/n\ZZ \right) / \langle m \rangle \cong
            \ZZ/\frac{n}{m}\ZZ
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I'll only give the sketch of the proof here. Lagrange's
          theorem says that the quotient group here must have size
          <m>\frac{n}{m}</m> and the cosets are represented by the
          numbers from 0 to <m>\frac{n}{m} - 1</m>. The isomorphism
          sends these cosets to the matching element of the integers
          modulo <m>\frac{n}{m}</m>. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Structure of Abelian Groups</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="proposition-cyclic-structure"/>, I proved a
      structure theorem for cyclic groups. I want to expand that
      theorem to all finitely generated abelian groups.  In the
      literature, this result gets referred to as a <sq>fundamental
      theorem</sq>. 
    </p>
    <theorem>
      <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Finitely-Generated Abelian
      Group</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a finitely generated abelian group. Then
          there are natural numbers <m>d, k_i \in \NN</m> and prime
          numbers (not necessarily distinct) <m>p_i</m> for some
          finite index set <m>i \in I</m> such that the following
          isomorphism exists. 
          <me>
            G \cong \ZZ^d \times \ZZ/p_1^{k_1} \times \ldots \times
            \ZZ/p_l^{k_l}
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          There are finitely many generators for the groups, so I will
          use induction on the number of generators. If there is one
          generator, then the group is cyclic and <xref
          ref="proposition-cyclic-structure"/> gives the required
          structure. That completes the base case.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now assume that the desired isomorphism exists for a group
          with up to <m>n</m> generators. I need to prove the
          isomorphism works for <m>n+1</m> generators. Let <m>g \in
          G</m> be one of the generators. Then consider the subgroup
          <m>\langle g \rangle</m>. This is a cyclic subgroup, either
          finite or infinite. Since <m>G</m> is abelian, all subgroups
          are normal. This means I can consider the quotient group
          <m>G/\langle g \rangle</m>. 
        </p>
        <p>
          I claim that this quotient group has <m>n</m> generators,
          which are precisely the remaining generators of <m>G</m>.
          Any element of <m>G</m> is a product of the generator, and
          in the quotient, all powers of <m>g</m> are sent to the
          identity; so the remaining generators create all the coset
          representatives. Therefore, the remaining generators
          generate the quotient group.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since the qoutient group has <m>n</m> generators, I can
          assume that it has the desired isomorophism by the induction
          assumption. Then I claim the following isomorphism.
          <me>
            G \cong \langle g \rangle \times \left( G/\langle g
            \rangle \right) 
          </me>
          Any element of <m>G</m> can be written as <m>g^r h</m> where
          <m>h</m> is generated by the remaining generators. The the
          isomorphism is <m>\phi(g^rh) = (g^r, h)</m>. This is a
          homormophism because the group is abelian and I can always
          move the powers of g to the start of the product. (This 
          isomorphism is not necessarily true in general for
          non-abelian groups). The map is surjective by construction,
          and its kernel is only the identity of g, so it is also
          injective. Therefore, this is an isormophism.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then I combine this isormophism with the induction
          assumption and <xref ref="proposition-cyclic-structure"/>.
          The quotient group is already of the desired
          form, and the new group is a cyclic group, so it also has
          the desired form, either just as something isomorphic to
          <m>\ZZ</m> or a product of the integers modulo powers of
          primes. By grouping the pieces of the two products, 
          I conclude that <m>G</m> has the desired form and the proof
          is completed. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      Now I've proved a very nice structure theorem. I understand the
      structure of most commonly encountered abelian groups.
      (Infinitely generated abelian groups exists, and we even know
      some of them: <m>\QQ</m>, <m>\RR</m> and most of the function
      groups under addition qualify.) Understanding completely the
      finitely generated abelian groups is a really nice result, and a
      good example of what progress and major results look like in
      group theory.
    </p>
    <p>
      ADD: repeat the table of small groups, but now all abelian
      groups of order 12 or less
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Simple Groups</title>
    <p>
      I prove a pretty substantial structure theorem in the previous
      section. Now let me define a new type of group which I will use
      to state (but certainly.  not prove) a much more important and
      substantial structure theorem. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A group which contains no normal subgroup (other than the
          trivial subgroup and the group itself) is called a
          <term>simple</term> group. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Why are these called simple group? The following proposition
      shows why. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a simple group. Then the only extension of
          <m>G</m> is the extension formed from the maps
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \Id: G \rightarrow G
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \pi_G: G \rightarrow \{e_g\}
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If there were any other extension, then the extension would
          have defined a kernel in <m>G</m>. That kernel would have
          been a normal subgroup. But normal subgroups don't exist in
          simple groups, so the kernel must be all of <m>G</m> or the
          trivial subgroup. This means that the only maps that can
          exist are the two that I have stated. (Technically, this is
          only true up to isomorphism, but that is enough for the
          result to make sense.) 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      Simple groups can't be broken apart into smaller groups. They
      are not products of smaller groups. Extensions are the general
      way to put groups together and the proposition shows that simple
      groups don't have any non-trivial extensions. They are the
      building blocks of groups. 
    </p>
    <p>
      If I think about the structure of groups, I can now ask this
      question in a new way.
      <ul>
        <li>
          What are all the simple groups?
        </li>
        <li>
          What are all the ways of putting simple groups together to
          make other groups? 
        </li>
      </ul>
      For finite groups, this is exactly how group theory developed in
      the 20th century. (For infinite groups, the classification
      problem in general is completely intractable, but
      classifications of various types of infinite groups typically
      have very different flavours and ideas.) I can now state the
      major theorem. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <title>The Classification of Finite Simple Groups</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a finite simple group. Then <m>G</m> must be
          one of the following types.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <m>G \cong \ZZ/p\ZZ</m> for <m>p</m> some prime number.
              (These are the only abelian finite groups).
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>G \cong A_n</m> for <m>n \geq 5</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>G</m> is a <term>simple group of Lie type</term>.
              There are 16 infinite families of these groups. 
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>G</m> is one of the 26 <term>sporadic groups</term>, a
              selection of groups that don't fit into the infinite
              families.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          This proof is a major accomplishment of 20th century
          mathematics. It was almost entirely completed in 1983, when
          it was announced. (Several small errors were found and fixed
          over the next 30 years). A reasonable estimate guesses that
          this proof is spread out over 10,000 pages of mathematical
          papers. There is a project to make a more concise version of
          the proof, but even that project is already several books
          long. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      There is a lot to say here. First, let me talk about the
      alternating groups. These are simple for <m>n \geq 5</m>. There
      is a long history behind this result. I'm not going to get into
      any details, but this relates deeply to solving polynomial
      equations. You all know the quadratic equation: it solves any
      quadratic (at least if complex numbers are allowed due to negative
      discriminants). There is a similar equation for cubic and
      quartics. However, no such formula exist for polynomials of
      degree 5.
    </p>
    <p>
      How to mathematicians find these general formulae? There are
      many ways, but one of the ways is by looking at permutations of
      the roots of a polynomial. That brings in the symmetric groups,
      and it turns out that even premutations are important. So the
      structure of <m>A_n</m> is somehow important to the roots of
      polynomials. Finally, after a bunch of very strange mathematics,
      its turns out that solving with roots (like in the quadratic
      formula) relies on normal subgroups of <m>A_n</m>. Since
      <m>A_n</m> is simple for <m>n \geq 5</m>, there are no normal
      subgroups. This means there is no equation for polynomial
      roots, no solution by roots, for any degree five or greater.
      Very strange.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, let me briefly mention the <sq>groups of Lie type</sq>.
      These families all come from Lie theory, which is a branch of
      group theory that deals with groups which are also interesting
      geometric shapes. It's a deep and complex area of mathematics in
      itself. It's not easy to same much more about it at this point,
      but it's a broad and active part of research mathematics. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, we have the sporadic groups. These are fascinating: 26
      special groups which are simple but don't fit into any of the
      infinite families. These come from a variety of different places
      in mathematics<mdash/>there is no unifying structure of idea
      behind all 26. Let me briefly give you their common notations and
      their orders.
      <me>
        \begin{array}{l|l}
          \text{Notation} \amp \text{Order} \\
          \hline
          M_{11} \amp 7,920 \\
          M_{12} \amp 95,040 \\
          J_1 \amp 175,560 \\
          M_{22} \amp 443,520 \\
          J_2 \amp 604,800 \\
          M_{23} \amp 10,200,960 \\
          T \amp 17,971,200 \\
          HS \amp 44,342,000 \\
          J_3 \amp 50,232,960 \\
          M_{24} \amp 244,823,040  \\
          McL \amp 898,128,000 \\
          F_7 \amp 4,030,387,200 \\
          Ru \amp 145,926,144,000 \\
          Suz \amp 448,345,487,600 \\
          ON \amp 460,815,505,920 \\
          Co_3 \amp 495,766,656,000 \\
          Co_2 \amp 42,305,421,312,000 \\
          Fi_{22} \amp 64,561,751,654,400 \\
          F_5 \amp 273,030,912,000,000 \\
          Ly \amp 51,765,179,004,000,000 \\
          F_3 \amp 90,745,943,887,872,000 \\
          Fi_{23} \amp 4,089,470,473,293,004,800 \\
          Co_1 \amp 4,157,776,806,543,360,000 \\
          J_4 \amp 86,775,571,046,007,562,880  \\
          Fi_{24} \amp 1,255,205,709,190,661,721,292,800 \\
          F_2 \amp 4,154,781,481,226,426,191,117,580,544,000,000 \\
          M \amp
          808,017,424,794,512,875,886,459,904,761,710,757,004,754,368,000,000,000
        \end{array}
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      This last group is called, rather dramatically, <term>the
      Monster</term>. Its existence was first proved in a paper
      published in 1980. In that paper, the only way to talk about
      this group is as a subgroup of <m>GL(196,883)</m>. Let me make it
      clear what is being said here: each element of the group is an
      invertible <m>196,883 \times 196,833</m> matrix and a
      transformation of the vector space <m>\RR^{196883}</m>. So each
      element is a matrix with <m>196,883^2 = 38,762,915,689</m>
      coefficients. Then there are approximately <m>8 \times
      10^{53}</m> of these matrices. This is, apparently, a group and,
      even with all these elements, it has no normal subgroups. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is wild, but even more can be said about the Monster. It
      can be proved that <m>196,883</m> is, indeed, the smallest
      possible dimension for which you can realize the Monster group
      as a group of matrices. So this number is somehow interesting.
      Over a decade after the construction of the Monster was
      published, other mathematicians were working in a completely
      distinct part of mathematics: modular functions. This is a part
      of (very advanced) calculus and geometry, and not at all related
      to group theory. The mathematicians were working with a
      function and, at one point, they calculated its Taylor
      expansion. This is what they wrote down.
      <me>
        J(q) = \frac{1}{q} + 744 + 196,884q + \ldots 
      </me>
      Now, <m>196,884</m> is only one different from <m>196,883</m>
      and they are both large enough numbers that this similarity
      might be notable. After a great deal of work, a very mysterious
      and deep connection was discovered. This was not a coincidence:
      there was a mathematical connection between the Monster and
      these modular functions. This is a very celebrated example of
      uncanny and unexpected conections in mathematics. It is ofte
      referred to as <term>monstrous moonshine</term>. You can even
      read a whole book on the subject, if you want: Moonshine beyond
      the Monster by Terry Gannon, a professor at the University of
      Alberta here in Edmonton. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Quotients and Extensions</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="definition-extension"/>, I defined extensions
      and talked about how extensions were a general way, using
      homomorphisms, to talk about putting two groups together to make
      a larger group. Now I want to relate quotients to extension.
      First, I need an important property of quotients.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\phi:K \rightarrow H</m> be a surjective homomorphism
          of groups. There there exists the following isormorphism
          <me>
            K/\Ker(\phi) \cong H
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I define the homomorphisms this way. Take a coset <m>k
          \Ker(\phi) \in K/\Ker(\phi)</m>. The function is defined as 
          <me>
            \eta(k \Ker(\phi)) = \phi(k) 
          </me>
          I need to prove several things: this is well-defined, the
          homomorhpisms property, and bijectivity
        </p>
        <p>
          First, well-defined. Cosets can have multiple
          representatives. If <m>k_1 \Ker(\phi)</m> and <m>k_2
          \Ker(\phi)</m> are two representatives of the same coset,
          then there eixsts some element <m>g</m> in the kernel such
          that <m>k_1 = k_2g</m>. Then I apply the function and use
          this identity.
          <me>
            \eta(k_1 \Ker(\phi)) = \phi(k_1) = \phi(k_2g)
          </me>
          Then I use the homomorphism property of <m>\phi</m>
          <me>
            \phi(k_2g) = \phi(k_2) \phi(g) 
          </me>
          But <m>g</m> is in the kernel, so it is sent to the identity
          <me>
            \phi(k_2) \phi(g) = \phi(k_2) = \eta(k_2 \Ker(\phi))
          </me>
          This proves that the function is well defined<mdash/>no
          matter what coset representative I use, the output is the
          same. 
        </p>
        <p>
          The other properties are a bit more immediate. Since
          <m>\eta</m> is defined by another homomorphism, I get the
          homomorphism property pretty easily. 
          <me>
            \eta(k_1 \Ker(\phi) k_2 (\Ker(\phi)) = \eta(k_1k_2
            \Ker(\phi) = \phi(k_1k_2) = \phi(k_1) \phi(k_2) = \eta(k_1
            \Ker(\phi)) \eta(k_2 \Ker(\phi))
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\eta</m> is surjective by construction: every value of
          <m>K</m> can show up as a coset representative, so <m>\eta(k
          \Ker(\phi)) = \phi(k)</m> is in the image for any <m>k</m>.
          Since <m>\phi</m> is surjective, <m>\eta</m> is also
          surjective.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lastly, what is the kernel of <m>\eta</m>? Well, to be sent
          to the identity, <m>\eta(k \Ker(\phi)) = \phi(k)</m> must be
          the identity. This means that <m>k \in \Ker (\phi)</m>, so
          <m>k \Ker(\phi)</m> must be the trivial coset, which is the
          identity in the quotient groups. Therefore, the kernel is
          just the identity and <m>\eta</m> is injetive. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Having proved all the conditions, I conlude that the desired
          isomorphism exists. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      With that technical detail finished, I can move on to the main
      idea of this section.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          There is a perfect matching (up to isormorphism) between
          extensions where (<m>K</m> is an extension of <m>H</m> by
          <m>G</m> and quotients of the group <m>K</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Assume that I have an extension with the notation from the
          statement of the theorem. Well then, by the definition of an
          extension, I have a surjective homomorphism <m>\pi: K
          \rightarrow H</m>. The kernel <m>\pi
        </p>
        <p>
          For the alternative, assume I have a surjective homomorphism
          <m>\pi: K \rightarrow H</m>. The <m>\Ker(\pi)</m> is a
          subgroup of <m>K</m>. I can define the inclusion map 
          <me>
            i: \Ker(\pi) \rightarrow K
          </me>
          which is just the identity on <m>\Ker(\pi)</m>. The image of
          this map is exactly <m>\Ker(\pi)</m> and the map, since it
          is the identity on the subgroup is injective. Therefore, I
          have an injective map 
          <me>
            i: \Ker(\pi) \rightarrow K
          </me>
          with image the same as the kernel of <m>\pi</m>. Therefore,
          these two maps defined <m>K</m> as an extension of <m>H</m>
          by <m>\Ker(\pi)</m>. 
        </p>
        <p>
          This defines a matching. The matching is up to isormorphism
          since the injection <m>i: G \rightarrow K</m> doesn't
          require that <m>G</m> is exactly the kernel of <m>\pi</m>,
          but just that it is isomorphic to that kernel. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      I defined products and extensions as a way of putting groups
      together. With this proposition, I can use the language of
      quotients to talk about how to break down a group into smaller
      pieces. Anytime a group has a normal subgroup, I can break the
      group down into an extension: the group can be decomposed into a
      normal subgroup and a quotient group by that normal subgroup.
      Normal subgroups break down groups into smaller pieces (which
      are then put back togehter using the language of group
      extensions.)
    </p>
    <p>
      This also lets me state a final theorem relating to the
      classification of finite simple groups. I need a technical
      result again first. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a finite group and let <m>N \triangleleft
          G</m> be a maximal normal subgroup. (Maximal here means that
          there is no larger normal subgroup which contains <m>N</m>.
          Then <m>G/N</m> is a simple group. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          For contradition, assume that <m>M \triangleleft G/N</m> is
          a non-trivial normal subgroup (not the identity or the whole
          group). The notation <m>\pi_N^{-1}(M)</m> is the pre-image
          of <m>M</m>. This is a subgroup of <m>G</m>: I can basically
          pull back all the group properties from <m>M</m>. (I'm being
          a bit informal here, but feel free to verify the details if
          you wish.) 
        </p>
        <p>
          Now I have two homomorphisms: <m>\pi_N G \righatrow G/N</m>
          and <m>\pi_M: G/N \rightarrow (G/N)/M</m>. I can compose
          these to get <m>\pi_M \circ \pi_N: G \rightarrow
          (G/N)/M</m>. The kernel of this composition are those things
          in <m>G</m> which gets set to <m>M \subset G/N</m>, since
          <m>M</m> is sent to the identity in the second quotients.
          Therefor <m>\pi_N^{-1}(M)</m> is precisely the kernel. (This
          also proves the subgroup property, since a kernel is always
          a subgroup.) 
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore <m>\pi_n^{-1}(M)</m> is a normal subgroup og
          <m>G</m> which is not all of <m>G</m> but which is larger
          than <m>N</m>. This is a contradiction to <m>N</m> being
          maximal. Therefore, I conclude, by contradiction that
          <m>G/N</m> must be simple.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      Now here is the final classification result here: a scheme that
      can, in principle at least, decribe how all finite groups are
      constructed.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a finite groups. Then <m>G</m> can be
          entirely constructed by finite simple groups and group
          extensions. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>G</m> is already simple, then I am done. Otherwise, I
          choose a maximal normal subgroup <m>N \triangleleft G</m>
          and make the quotient <m>G/N</m>. Then I know that <m>G</m>
          is an extension of <m>G/N</m> by <m>N</m>. I also know that
          <m>G/N</m> is a simple group. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Now I iterate using <m>N</m> instead of <m>G</m>. I look for
          a maximal normal subgroup, take the quotient, and get an
          extension where the quotient is simple. I repeat the
          iteration until I get an extension where both parts are
          simple; this will always happen since the original group is
          finite and the quotients keep getting smaller. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      So there you have it: a way to understand all finite groups. You
      can start with simple groups and keep making extensions using
      more simple groups. In this way, you can build any groups.
      Equivalently, you can start with a finite groups, take quotients
      that are simple, and keep repeating until you just have a
      complete sequence of simple quotients. In this way, all finite
      groups are constructed from the simple groups. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>

